# Runbook — Figma library push

> This runbook is invoked by Claude Code's `/figma-library-push` slash command
> (via `.claude/skills/figma-library-push/SKILL.md`), by Cursor's always-apply
> rule at `.cursor/rules/agents.mdc` when the user's intent matches, and by any
> other agent pointed here from `AGENTS.md`. The procedure is identical
> whichever agent invokes it. It is also invoked from the sync-tokens runbook
> as its Figma step.

Create or update the project's Figma design library — variable collections,
modes, aliases, scopes, code syntax, descriptions, text styles, token-sheet
frames — from the canonical design-system files (`DESIGN.md` for identity and,
on a web project, `IMPLEMENT.web.md` for Large-mode behaviour). Verify by
read-back and write `tokens.lock.json`.

Writes the design-system values layer into the project's Figma file, through the
**official Figma MCP** (claude.ai connector) — no desktop plugin needed. Everything
here is idempotent: inspect first, update what exists, create only what's missing,
never duplicate.

The mechanics below — collections, modes, aliases, scopes, code syntax,
mode-pinned frames — were verified end-to-end in 2026-07. Variable
`description` is a writable Plugin API string (same class as
`hiddenFromPublishing`); it was not exercised in that spike. Write it anyway;
if the assignment throws, skip descriptions and continue. The **shape** those
mechanics are applied to changed twice since: in 2026-08 when DESIGN.md became
canonical (no primitives collection, a two-mode Scale collection); and again
when identity and behaviour split into DESIGN.md and IMPLEMENT.web.md
(Large-mode values now come from IMPLEMENT.web.md's `responsive` block rather
than from `-lg` suffixed twins in DESIGN.md). Individual calls are proven; this
particular arrangement of them has not had a full run yet. Treat step 5's
read-back as load-bearing.

## Before any write

1. Load the Figma **figma-use** guidance: the `/figma-use` skill if installed, else
   read `skill://figma/figma-use/SKILL.md` via the Figma MCP, plus
   `references/variable-patterns.md` (and `text-style-patterns.md` when creating text
   styles). Non-negotiable — it prevents the known failure modes.
2. `whoami` → note plan tier. Starter tier allows **1 mode per collection**, which
   collides with the two layout modes. On a starter-tier file, push the base
   (Small) values only, skip every Large-mode override from IMPLEMENT.web.md,
   note the limitation in DESIGN.md, and tell the operator — Large is then a
   code-only concern until the file is upgraded.
3. Read `DESIGN.md` — the front matter is the identity token set, including any
   trailing `#` comments that become Figma variable descriptions — and, on a
   web project, `IMPLEMENT.web.md` — its `responsive` block is the Large-mode
   source. Read `tokens.lock.json` if present. Resolve the **active phase**
   first; everything below reads and writes that phase's entry and nothing else.
4. Inspect the file: existing collections, variables, text styles
   (`getLocalVariableCollectionsAsync` etc.). Match existing conventions.

## Library-first: when the file already has variables

If step 4 above found existing variable collections with defined values, this file
came from a starter kit or boilerplate. The procedure below still applies, but the
**write strategy changes from "create" to "restructure in place."**

**Principles:**

- **Do not delete the boilerplate's collections or variables.** Restructure by
  renaming, moving, and adding — never by tearing down and rebuilding. Deleting
  variables breaks any components or frames in the file that reference them.
- **Rename** variables to match our naming convention. A variable named
  `Colors/neutral/100` becomes `surface/secondary` (or whatever the confirmed
  mapping says). Renaming preserves all existing bindings — any frame fill bound to
  that variable still works, it just resolves under the new name.
- **Reorganise into three collections** (Primitives, Colour, Scale) if the
  boilerplate uses a different structure. Move variables between collections where
  needed. If the boilerplate has a single flat collection, split it.
- **Add** our primitives layer, semantic aliases, scale structure, and mode
  configuration on top of the existing values. The starter kit may lack modes,
  scopes, code syntax, or descriptions — add what is missing without disturbing
  what works.
- **Preserve components and frames** that reference existing variables. The point
  of renaming rather than deleting is that the boilerplate's components (buttons,
  cards, inputs) stay functional with the new token names.
- **Record the original names** in `tokens.lock.json`'s `notes` array for
  traceability:
  ```json
  "notes": [
    "Library-first: restructured from 'Luxury E-com Design System Starter Kit'",
    "Renamed: Colors/primary/500 → primitive/kit-primary → colors.primary",
    "Renamed: Colors/neutral/100 → surface/secondary"
  ]
  ```

**The procedure steps below still run in order.** For each collection (Primitives,
Colour, Scale): if a variable already exists under its target name, update its
value and properties (scopes, code syntax, description). If a variable exists
under a boilerplate name that maps to this target, rename it first, then
update. If no variable exists for this target, create it fresh. The result is
the same three-collection structure whether the file started empty or
pre-populated.

## Write path (in order of preference)

1. **Official Figma MCP `use_figma`** — primary, proven for: collections, modes,
   COLOR/FLOAT/STRING variables, `VARIABLE_ALIAS`, scopes, `setVariableCodeSyntax`,
   text styles, mode-pinned frames, value updates. Variable `description` uses the
   same path (`variable.description = "…"`); not spike-verified, see below.
2. **figma-console MCP** (if the user has it) — fallback only. Known quirks: verify
   `figma.fileKey` before any write (navigate lies); delete via `figma_delete_node`.
3. **Manual fallback** — never dead-end: print a formatted token sheet (name, value
   per mode, scope, code syntax, description) the operator can enter by hand, plus
   the checklist.

## Variable descriptions

Figma's per-variable **Description** field ("How to use this variable") is
writable: `variable.description = "…"`. Set it on every create **and** every
update — the same overwrite-from-code rule as values. A description typed
directly in Figma does not survive the next push.

**Source:** a trailing `#` comment on that token's YAML line in `DESIGN.md`.
The design.md schema has no per-token description field, and a new YAML key
would trip the linter. A comment is ignored by lint and by CSS emission, and
is readable to an agent parsing the file as text.

```yaml
state-hover: "rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.04)"  # Neutral 4% overlay. Fill on interactive surfaces, not a text colour.
```

For a composite token (a typography entry, an `extensions` group), the comment
sits on the parent key:

```yaml
display:  # Hero type. Grows at Large; the override lives in IMPLEMENT.web.md.
  fontFamily: …
```

A nested property with its own comment wins over the parent for the Figma
variable derived from that property (`type/display/size` reads the `fontSize`
line, not `display:`).

**Rules:**

- Strip the `#` and trim. One sentence. This is a usage hint in a dense table,
  not a dump of the DESIGN.md section.
- No comment → set `description` to `""`. Do not invent a line from the prose.
  Empty is honest; a guessed sentence is a second source of truth.
- Descriptions are per-variable, not per-mode. Scale tokens with Small and
  Large values share one description, sourced from DESIGN.md (identity), never
  from IMPLEMENT.web.md.
- Code syntax remains the name bridge. Description is metadata and is never
  matched on.
- Same `use_figma` path as `setVariableCodeSyntax`. If the assignment throws,
  skip descriptions, record it in the lock `notes`, and continue — values,
  scopes and code syntax still land.
- `get_variable_defs` does **not** return descriptions. After the value
  read-back in step 7, run a small read-only `use_figma` script that returns
  `{ codeSyntax, description }` for local variables and check them against the
  DESIGN.md comments. A description-only mismatch is reported, not a failed
  push.

## Procedure (small incremental calls — ≤10 logical ops each, return IDs every call)

DESIGN.md has a `primitives` block (raw brand values) and a `colors` block (semantic
roles that alias into primitives). Three collections cover both files.

1. **Primitives collection** — **single mode**, named "Primitives". One variable per
   `primitives.*` key: `brand-red` → `primitive/brand-red`. All values are literal
   (no aliases — primitives are the bottom of the reference chain). Scopes: none
   (primitives are referenced by semantic tokens, not used directly in designs).
   **Code syntax always**: `WEB → var(--primitive-<kebab-name>)`.
   **Description** from the YAML `#` comment, or `""`.
2. **Colour collection** — **single mode**, named for the project's actual mode
   (usually "Light", but name it for what the brand is, not what is assumed). One
   variable per `colors.*` key: `fg-primary` → `fg/primary`, `surface-inverse` →
   `surface/inverse`. A `"{primitives.x}"` value becomes a `VARIABLE_ALIAS` to the
   corresponding Primitives collection variable; a `"{colors.x}"` value becomes a
   `VARIABLE_ALIAS` within this collection; a literal stays literal. Scopes by role:
   `FRAME_FILL, SHAPE_FILL` for `surface/*`, `TEXT_FILL` for `fg/*`, both for
   `primary` and `accent`, `STROKE_COLOR` for `line/*`. The three `state/*` overlays
   and `focus-ring` get all fill scopes — they land anywhere. **Code syntax always**:
   `WEB → var(--<kebab-path>)`, the name anchor sync-tokens matches on.
   **Description** from the YAML `#` comment, or `""`.
3. **Scale collection** — **two modes, "Small" and "Large"**, holding `spacing`,
   `rounded`, and the numeric half of `typography`. Base (Small) values come
   from `DESIGN.md`; Large-mode values come from `IMPLEMENT.web.md`'s
   `responsive` block. Mapping is described below. Scopes: `GAP, WIDTH_HEIGHT`
   for `space/*`, `CORNER_RADIUS` for `radius/*`, `FONT_SIZE` / `LINE_HEIGHT` /
   `LETTER_SPACING` for type. Code syntax as above. **Description** from the
   YAML `#` comment, or `""`.
4. **Text styles**: one per `typography` entry in `DESIGN.md`, named exactly
   like the key. Bind `fontSize`, `lineHeight` and `letterSpacing` to their
   Scale variables with `setBoundVariable`, so a single style resolves at both
   widths. There are no `-lg` type styles — the Large-mode values come from
   the Scale variables' Large mode, which is sourced from IMPLEMENT.web.md.
   Follow the canonical font recipe: `listAvailableFontsAsync` to verify the
   family/style string, `loadFontAsync`, then create. Brand font not available in
   Figma → build the style with Inter at the correct size, and add "swap text styles
   to <font> after installing it in Figma" to the human checklist.
   *Unverified as of 2026-08:* if `setBoundVariable` on a text style fails, fall
   back to two flat styles (`display` for Small, `display@large` for Large sourced
   from IMPLEMENT.web.md), note it on the checklist, and record which path was
   taken in the lock file's `notes`.
5. **Token sheets** (the styleguide + machine read-point): three frames, split by
   what varies:
   - **"Tokens — Primitives"** — one row per primitive variable (swatch bound to the
     variable + name label). Single frame, no mode complexity — primitives have one
     mode.
   - **"Tokens — Colour"** — one row per semantic colour variable (swatch + name +
     alias annotation showing which primitive it points to). Single frame — colours
     do not change by size.
   - **"Tokens — Scale · Small"** — one row per scale/spacing/rounded variable, one
     line per text style, all pinned to Small mode.
   Clone → **"Tokens — Scale · Large"** →
   `setExplicitVariableModeForCollection(scaleCollection, largeModeId)`.

   The split makes it visually obvious that colour is size-independent and only scale
   values have two modes. Screenshot all four sheets.
6. **Specimen sheets** — designer-facing reference pages that show the design system
   in context rather than as raw values. Create after the token sheets (which are
   machine read-points), on a separate page named "Specimens". Layout reference:
   `figma.com/design/ef1Dz0cIb3jcch5izjk2db/Templates?node-id=1-171`.

   Three specimen frames:

   **Colour specimens** — a grid of colour swatches, each showing:
   - A filled rectangle bound to the semantic colour variable
   - The token name (`primary`, `surface-secondary`)
   - The primitive it aliases to (`→ brand-red`)
   - The resolved hex value
   - For `fg-*` tokens: a text sample on its natural background
   - For contrast pairs: the contrast ratio (e.g. "4.8:1")

   **Type specimens** — one row per typography style showing:
   - The style name (`display`, `heading-1`, `body`)
   - A sample sentence set in that style (use TONE-OF-VOICE.md register, not lorem)
   - Size / weight / line-height annotation
   - Both Small and Large sizes side by side (for styles that grow)

   **Spacing and shape specimens** — a visual ruler showing:
   - Each spacing step as a labelled gap
   - The three radii applied to sample rectangles
   - Small vs Large comparisons for steps that grow

   Build these programmatically using `use_figma`. Bind swatches and text to the
   actual variables so specimens stay live when tokens change. The token sheets
   (step 5) are the machine verification layer; specimens are the human review
   layer. Both exist.

7. **Verify**: `get_variable_defs` on each token sheet node — every token must come
   back, keyed by its code syntax, with the right resolved value per mode. Then a
   small read-only `use_figma` script returning `{ codeSyntax, description }` for
   local variables, checked against the DESIGN.md `#` comments. A
   description-only mismatch is reported, not a failed push.
8. **Write `tokens.lock.json`** beside `DESIGN.md`, into the active phase's entry
   only. Shape and rules below. Timestamp from `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ`.

## Responsive values live in IMPLEMENT.web.md

The Scale collection still has two modes — Small and Large. What changed in
2026-08 is where each mode's value comes from. **Base (Small) values come from
`DESIGN.md`; Large-mode values come from `IMPLEMENT.web.md`'s `responsive`
block.** One variable, two modes, two sources.

The mapping:

| Source | Figma variable | Mode |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `DESIGN.md → spacing.lg: 24px` | `space/lg` | Small |
| `IMPLEMENT.web.md → responsive.spacing.lg."@large": 32px` | `space/lg` — same variable | Large |
| `DESIGN.md → spacing.md: 16px` (no responsive entry) | `space/md` | **both**, identical value |
| `DESIGN.md → typography.display.fontSize: 33px` | `type/display/size` | Small |
| `IMPLEMENT.web.md → responsive.typography.display."@large".fontSize: 48px` | `type/display/size` — same variable | Large |

Absence in `responsive` is meaningful: no override entry means the value is
deliberately the same at both widths, so write the base value to both modes
rather than leaving Large unset. An unset mode inherits nothing in Figma — it
resolves empty and the sheet read-back in step 5 will catch it, which is the
point of doing the read-back.

## `tokens.lock.json`

Machine-maintained, never hand-edited, and read only by tooling — the
sync-tokens runbook diffs against it and `/project-check` reports on it. It is
the record of what Figma actually holds, which is a different question from
what DESIGN.md says.

**Keyed by phase**, because a project can hold a frozen concepting library and a
live detailed-design one at the same time, in different Figma files:

```json
{
  "_meta": {
    "schema": "prototype-tokens-lock-v3",
    "activePhase": "detailed-design"
  },
  "phases": {
    "concepting": {
      "frozen": true,
      "frozenAt": "2026-06-30T14:02:11Z",
      "designTag": "design/concepting-final",
      "fileKey": "aBc123…",
      "collections": { "primitives": "VariableCollectionId:1:1", "colour": "VariableCollectionId:1:2", "scale": "VariableCollectionId:1:9" },
      "modes": { "Primitives": "1:0", "Light": "2:0", "Small": "9:0", "Large": "9:1" },
      "variables": { "primitives.brand-red": "VariableID:3:10", "colors.primary": "VariableID:3:41", "spacing.lg": "VariableID:3:88" },
      "textStyles": { "display": "S:7f2a…" },
      "tokenSheets": { "Primitives": "12:100", "Colour": "12:200", "Scale·Small": "12:340", "Scale·Large": "12:512" },
      "values": {
        "primitives.brand-red": { "Primitives": "#E8112D" },
        "colors.primary": { "Light": "#E8112D" },
        "spacing.lg": { "Small": "24px", "Large": "32px" },
        "spacing.md": { "Small": "16px", "Large": "16px" }
      },
      "pushedAt": "2026-06-30T13:58:02Z",
      "notes": []
    },
    "detailed-design": {
      "frozen": false,
      "fileKey": "xYz789…",
      "…": "same shape, no frozenAt or designTag"
    }
  }
}
```

`values` is the **full snapshot**, every token keyed by path then by mode, and it
is what makes a three-way diff possible: without the previous values, a change in
Figma and a change in code are indistinguishable from each other.

Tokens listed under `spacing.lg` carry both modes even though the two mode
values come from two different files (base from DESIGN.md, Large from
IMPLEMENT.web.md). The lock file records Figma's shape, not either source
file's.

### Freezing a phase

At a phase boundary the outgoing library stops being a live surface:

1. Tag the repo at the current state of the design-system files (DESIGN.md,
   and IMPLEMENT.web.md on a web project) — `design/<phase>-final` — and
   record the tag as `designTag`. This is the point of the tag: a year later
   the frozen library and the files that produced it can still be lined up.
2. Set `frozen: true` and `frozenAt` to the tag time.
3. Point `_meta.activePhase` at the new phase and create its entry, pushing to a
   **new Figma file**. Freezing does not mean editing in place.

**Never write to a frozen entry, and never report drift against one.** A frozen
library diverging from DESIGN.md or IMPLEMENT.web.md is the expected and
correct end state — the source files keep moving and the library does not.
Reporting that as drift trains people to ignore drift reports, which costs
more than the report is worth.

## Known failure modes (learned the hard way — don't relearn)

- **The remote MCP's font list is NOT the user's font list.** `listAvailableFontsAsync`
  in the connector environment doesn't see fonts installed on the operator's machine
  or uploaded to their org — brand faces will read as missing even when designers use
  them daily in the same file. Expect the Inter-placeholder path to be the norm, not
  the exception; the swap always goes on the human checklist.

- **Mode ids belong to their own collection.** `setValueForMode` with another
  collection's mode id throws `mode with id … not defined for variable`. Always
  resolve the mode id from the variable's own `variableCollectionId`.
- Failed `use_figma` scripts are **atomic** — nothing applied. Read the error, fix,
  retry once; don't blind-retry.
- COLOR variable values take `{r,g,b,a}` (0–1); paints take `{r,g,b}`. Don't mix.
- `"Semi Bold"` not `"SemiBold"` — verify font style strings, never guess.

## Always-human steps (no API exists — print, don't attempt)

- Installing/uploading brand fonts into Figma (org font upload is an admin action).
- **Publishing the library** so other project files can use it (Figma UI button).
- Restyling the token sheets to taste.
